Autonomous Zero‑Boil‑Off Cryogenic Propellant Storage for Crewed Mars Return:

Theory, Variable‑Density Insulation, and Reinforcement‑Learning Control

Author
Ouadi Maakoul

Abstract

A liquid‑oxygen/liquid‑methane return stage from Mars demands a zero‑boil‑off (ZBO) capability over a 500‑day interplanetary coast, without which passive boil‑off would erode the propellant reserve to mission‑ending levels. This dissertation develops a complete theoretical and architectural framework that guarantees ZBO for multi‑tonne tanks through the tight integration of optimised multilayer insulation, stress‑compliant structural cooling, and a provably safe autonomous controller.

The thermal‑fluid foundation is a Dalton–Henry coupled thermodynamic model that correctly captures the influence of helium pressurant on condensation, pre‑structured for validation against the ZBOT‑NC flight experiment. On the insulation side, the McIntosh layer‑by‑layer radiative‑conduction model is combined with simulated annealing and a Proper Orthogonal Decomposition–Gaussian Radial‑Basis Function surrogate to design a variable‑density multilayer insulation blanket that yields an 18 % heat‑leak reduction over a uniform blanket of identical mass. An Integrated Tube‑on‑Shield strut, incorporating 3D‑printed active cooling channels and a Teflon‑based expansion‑mismatch slip layer, reduces the conductive heat leak tenfold. A dual‑redundant pulse‑tube cryocooler architecture with cross‑strapped helium loops achieves a system reliability greater than 0.95, and the total active‑system mass is shown to be recovered within 60 days of flight for the 111 t Mars‑return stage, after which the system becomes a net propellant saver.

The capstone is a Soft Actor‑Critic reinforcement‑learning agent, trained on a reduced‑order digital twin validated against ZBOT‑1 microgravity data, and protected by a Lyapunov‑based safety shield that provably prevents over‑pressure. The agent outperforms model‑predictive control by 22 % in cumulative reward under unmodelled heat‑flux spikes, demonstrating anticipatory pre‑cooling strategies that linear controllers cannot replicate. The architecture therefore provides the mathematical, thermal, and autonomous‑control blueprint for the first space‑based propellant depot—the critical link that renders in‑situ resource utilisation and crewed Mars return physically viable.



Chapter 1

The Boil‑Off Crisis and the Zero‑Boil‑Off Imperative

A liquid‑oxygen/liquid‑methane return stage from Mars demands a specific impulse I_{sp}\approx 370 \, \text{s} and a total velocity change \Delta v \approx 5.6 \, \text{km}\,\text{s}^{-1}. The classical rocket equation

\frac{m_0}{m_f} = \exp\left(\frac{\Delta v}{g_0 I_{sp}}\right) \approx 4.7

forces an initial propellant mass of roughly 111\, \text{t} for a 30\, \text{t} payload.
Passive thermal protection alone is insufficient. Even for methane and oxygen, which have lower boil‑off rates than hydrogen, modern multilayer insulation (MLI) and vapour‑cooled shields typically yield a boil‑off rate on the order of 0.1\,\% to 0.3\,\% of total mass per day. Adopting the conservative end of that range, 0.1\% per day, after a 500-day interplanetary coast the remaining propellant is

m_p(500) = 111\,000 \times 0.999^{500} \approx 8\,880\ \text{kg},

a mission‑ending loss that eliminates the capacity to transport crew or cargo. Zero‑Boil‑Off (ZBO) storage—active cooling that precisely compensates the total heat leak at every instant—is therefore not a design option but a physical requirement for crewed Mars return.

The urgency is amplified by the role of cryogenic storage in in‑situ resource utilisation (ISRU). Liquid oxygen and methane produced on the Martian surface must be accumulated and preserved for the return window, often for more than a year. The technology described in this dissertation is the critical link that would make such an ISRU‑enabled architecture viable: it is, in essence, the “first space gas station.”

A widespread misconception holds that the vacuum of space eliminates boil‑off; in reality, it is the combination of radiative loading from the Sun and planetary bodies and conductive heat leaks through structural supports that drives evaporation. The following chapters quantify these mechanisms, derive optimal mitigation techniques, and culminate in a reinforcement‑learning control architecture that autonomously ensures ZBO.

The central question is whether a hybrid passive‑active system—comprising optimised variable‑density multilayer insulation, stress‑compliant structural cooling, and an autonomous reinforcement‑learning controller—can guarantee ZBO for multi‑tonne Mars‑return tanks with a reliability exceeding 0.95 over a 87\,600-hour lifetime.

