Chapter 2

Foundations of Cryogenic Fluid Management: Physics, Models, and Gaps

This chapter surveys the scientific landscape, identifies the precise gaps in the current state of the art, and defines the scope of original contributions that follow.

NASA’s cryogenic fluid management heritage
From the passive Apollo Service Module tanks to the ill‑fated Shuttle Centaur, the historical record teaches a clear lesson: no purely passive insulation can maintain ZBO for more than a few weeks. The breakthrough came with the Zero‑Boil‑Off Tank (ZBOT) experiment series. The first flight article, ZBOT‑1, was launched to the International Space Station and collected long‑duration microgravity pressure‑rise data during 2017–2018. That dataset demonstrated unequivocally that slosh and the presence of helium pressurant gas drastically alter local evaporation and condensation rates, even in a single‑component system. While ZBOT‑1 validated computational fluid dynamics (CFD) models for pure cryogens, it left the influence of a non‑condensable gas (NCG) largely unaddressed.

ZBOT‑NC, the follow‑on experiment specifically designed to study helium dissolution, stratified condensation, and pressure control with NCGs, was launched to the ISS aboard the NG‑23 Cygnus resupply mission in 2025. Its data, expected to be publicly released within the next two years, will provide the first microgravity validation of two‑component phase‑change models. The ground‑based LOXSAT/CPST campaigns previously proved that a large tank can achieve ZBO when a pulse‑tube cryocooler is actively controlled, but all these efforts relied on classical model‑predictive control and did not examine the potential of learning‑based autonomy.

Multilayer insulation modelling
The Lockheed correlation, though widely cited in preliminary design, was fitted to data above 77 K and systematically underestimates heat leak at liquid‑oxygen/liquid‑methane temperatures. The layer‑by‑layer (LBL) radiative‑conduction model of McIntosh (1996), which solves the coupled energy balance for every shield and spacer, delivers \pm 5\% accuracy at 90–111 K and is therefore adopted as the sole MLI analysis tool in this work. Recent literature has extended the LBL framework to variable‑density MLI (VDMLI) and has demonstrated that optimising the layer density can reduce heat leak by 21 % to 25 % for liquid‑hydrogen temperatures. No comparable optimisation has yet been carried out for a methane/oxygen stage using a formal global search algorithm with a high‑fidelity surrogate model—a gap that Chapter 4 addresses directly.

Cryocooler technology
Two cryocooler types dominate the flight‑proven candidates for multi‑year, deep‑space operation: the Creare 20 W@90 K pulse‑tube cryocooler and the Northrop Grumman High‑Efficiency Cryocooler (HEC). Both employ pulse‑tube thermoacoustic cycles with no moving parts at the cold head, yielding lifetimes in excess of ten years. Their coefficient of performance at 90 K is approximately 0.05–0.10. The present architecture assumes dual‑redundant units with cross‑strapped helium circulation loops, a configuration that a failure modes, effects, and criticality analysis (FMECA) shows yields a system reliability greater than 0.95.

Microgravity mixing
NASA now identifies subcooled droplet injection via a spray bar as one of the “promising” methods for suppressing thermal stratification and preventing geysers. Numerical work published in 2025 demonstrated that a three‑dimensional VOF (Volume‑of‑Fluid) model with the Schrage phase‑change source term can accurately reproduce the mixing and cooling characteristics of buoyant jets from a spray‑bar thermodynamic vent system. A parallel study specifically modelled “Self‑Pressurization and Spray Bar Pressure Control of a Cryogenic Storage Tank” using VOF and the Schrage equation, directly validating the physics chosen for the digital twin in Chapter 6.

Structural‑thermal decoupling
Patent CN113864631B teaches the use of a Teflon‑impregnated slip layer between a structural strut and its foam insulation to survive the 4–10× coefficient of thermal expansion (CTE) mismatch between metal and cryogenic foam. This principle is incorporated directly into the Integrated Tube‑on‑Shield (ITOS) strut design developed in Chapter 5.

Autonomous control gap
Model‑predictive control (MPC) is the current NASA baseline for cryogenic pressure management. Reinforcement learning (RL), however, has demonstrated—in domains ranging from robotics to power electronics—the ability to discover pre‑emptive strategies that MPC, constrained by linearised model horizons, cannot anticipate. No RL controller has ever been designed, trained, or proven for a ZBO tank with non‑condensable gas dynamics. Filling that gap is the principal ambition of Chapter 7.

