Chapter 3

Thermodynamic and Transport Foundations with Noncondensable Gases

We begin with the global conservation laws for a closed ZBO tank containing liquid (l) and vapour (v) of a pure cryogen, together with a known mass of helium pressurant. The energy and mass balances are

\frac{d}{dt}\bigl(m_l u_l + m_v u_v\bigr) = Q_{\text{leak}} - \dot{Q}_{\text{cooler}},\qquad
\frac{d m_l}{dt} = -\frac{Q_{\text{leak}} - \dot{Q}_{\text{cooler}}}{h_{fg}},
\tag{3.1}

where h_{fg} is the latent heat of vaporisation. ZBO is defined by the invariance condition \dot{Q}_{\text{cooler}} \equiv Q_{\text{leak}} at all times, implying dm_l/dt = 0.

The total heat leak decomposes into three principal paths:

Q_{\text{leak}} = Q_{\text{rad}} + Q_{\text{cond}} + Q_{\text{pipe}},
\tag{3.2}

where Q_{\text{rad}} originates from the MLI blanket and is computed by the layer‑by‑layer model of Chapter 4, Q_{\text{cond}} is the conductive load through the ITOS struts (Chapter 5), and Q_{\text{pipe}} is a small, constant plumbing term.

Dalton–Henry coupling
With helium present, the total tank pressure is not the saturation pressure of the pure cryogen; it obeys Dalton’s law

P_{\text{tank}} = P_{\text{vap}}(T_{\text{int}}) + P_{\text{He}},
\tag{3.3}

where P_{\text{vap}} is the vapour pressure evaluated at the liquid‑vapour interface temperature T_{\text{int}}. The helium partial pressure is determined by a mass balance that couples the ullage gas with dissolved helium in the liquid. Assuming instantaneous local equilibrium, Henry’s law gives

m_{\text{He}} = \frac{P_{\text{He}} V_{\text{ullage}} M_{\text{He}}}{R_u T_{\text{ullage}}} + \frac{P_{\text{He}}}{\mathcal{H}(T)} m_l,
\tag{3.4}

with \mathcal{H}(T) the experimentally determined Henry constant for helium in liquid methane (or oxygen). Equation (3.4) is a nonlinear algebraic constraint that must be solved simultaneously with (3.1). When the cryocooler condenses vapour, a helium‑rich boundary layer accumulates on the condenser surface, reducing the effective condensation mass‑transfer coefficient. This blanketing effect is of precisely the type that the ZBOT‑NC experiment was designed to quantify; the present model will be validated against its data as soon as they become publicly available.

Lyapunov proof of bounded pressure
Consider the simplified system with state x = [P, T_l, T_v]^{\mathsf{T}} and a dead‑band controller that applies maximum cooling \dot{Q}_{c,\max} whenever P > P_{\text{set}}. If \dot{Q}_{c,\max} > Q_{\text{leak,max}}, then the Lyapunov function candidate

V = \frac{1}{2}(P - P_{\text{set}})^2

has a negative definite derivative along all trajectories, thereby guaranteeing that the pressure remains bounded and never exceeds the maximum expected operating pressure (MEOP). This classical argument is extended to the RL‑shielded system in Chapter 7 using the formal framework of Lyapunov‑based reinforcement learning. Recent advances such as the Critic as Lyapunov Function (CALF) algorithm and techniques for embedding Lyapunov stability conditions as inductive biases in deep neural networks provide rigorous tools for the safety proof presented there.

