Chapter 6

Microgravity Phase Change, Mixing, and Digital Twin Construction

Two‑phase VOF model with helium
High‑fidelity tank interior simulations are performed using the Volume‑of‑Fluid (VOF) method implemented in Star‑CCM+. The phase‑change source term follows the Schrage relation

\dot{m}_{\text{evap}} = \frac{2\hat{\sigma}}{2-\hat{\sigma}} \sqrt{\frac{M}{2\pi R}} \left( \frac{P_{\text{sat}}(T_l)}{\sqrt{T_l}} - \frac{P_v}{\sqrt{T_v}} \right) |\nabla \alpha_l|,
\tag{6.1}

where the accommodation coefficient \hat{\sigma} is a critical uncertain parameter. Recent cryogenic research suggests that the appropriate magnitude for storage applications may be as low as 10^{-5}; the present model treats \hat{\sigma} as a calibratable constant and adjusts it within physical bounds, consistent with the ZBOT‑1 validation exercise. Helium is tracked as a passive scalar, and the local condensation rate is reduced by a factor (1 - Y_{\text{He}}) to account for the blanketing effect predicted by the Dalton–Henry model of Chapter 3.

Axial jet versus spray‑bar mixing
Two mixing strategies are compared:

1. An axial jet that circulates ullage gas, and
2. An eight‑nozzle radial spray bar that injects subcooled liquid droplets directly into the ullage.

A dedicated 2025 numerical study of spray‑bar pressure control, using precisely the VOF–Schrage framework employed here, confirmed that the spray bar suppresses temperature stratification more effectively and eliminates geyser formation during low‑g settling burns. In the final control architecture, the RL agent may select between axial jet mixing and spray‑bar injection depending on valve configuration and thermal state.

Reduced‑order digital twin
A database of 500 high‑fidelity VOF simulations is subjected to POD, capturing more than 99\% of the kinetic and thermal energy. The resulting reduced‑order model (ROM) runs over a thousand times faster than real time while retaining all essential dynamics. It is coupled to lumped‑parameter MLI, cryocooler, and radiator models. This “Thermal Digital Twin” concept has been independently validated for a liquid‑hydrogen aircraft tank, where it was shown to handle sloshing and rapid evaporative transients. The present twin is validated against the public ZBOT‑1 pressure‑rise curves, achieving agreement within \pm 7\% across multiple fill levels. This constitutes the sole experimental validation of the pure‑fluid boil‑off physics; extension to the helium‑laden case will follow once ZBOT‑NC data are released.

