Chapter 7

Autonomous Cryo‑Management via Reinforcement Learning — Capstone

Problem formulation
The RL agent selects the cryocooler compressor power u \in [0, P_{\text{max}}] once per minute. The reward function combines vent‑reduction, energy economy, and a soft pressure‑limit:

r = -w_1 \dot{m}_{\text{vent}}^2 - w_2 u + w_3 \mathbf{1}_{P < P_{\text{set}}}.
\tag{7.1}

The state vector is 14‑dimensional: tank pressure, liquid and ullage temperatures, helium partial pressure, solar irradiance, available electrical power, cold‑head temperature, and time to next thermal event.

Safety shield
A deterministic override, implemented as a hard constraint, guarantees that whenever P > 0.95\,P_{\text{MEOP}} the cryocooler is commanded to 100\,\% power. Leveraging the Lyapunov framework introduced in Chapter 3, we prove that the closed‑loop system is Input‑to‑State Stable (ISS) with respect to the heat‑leak disturbance, and that tank pressure never exceeds MEOP. The proof draws on recent advances in Lyapunov‑based RL, including the Critic as Lyapunov Function (CALF) methodology and strategies for embedding Lyapunov conditions as inductive biases within the policy network architecture.

Algorithm and training
A Soft Actor‑Critic (SAC) agent is trained for one million episodes on the digital twin under extensive domain randomisation: heat flux \pm 30\%, sensor noise \pm 20\%, and random slosh events. The converged policy is then distilled into a compact feed‑forward network suitable for embedding on a flight‑qualified processor. This procedure is aligned with a growing body of work demonstrating the feasibility of model‑based RL for cryogenic tank management; a large European research consortium has recently begun a multi‑year project devoted explicitly to RL‑based optimal management of cryogenic tanks, controlling the coupled dynamics of sloshing and boil‑off. The present work appears to be the first to deliver a fully trained, safety‑shielded RL controller for a ZBO mission.

Quantitative comparison with MPC
A Model‑Predictive Controller (MPC) based on a linearised version of the twin serves as the benchmark. Under nominal conditions both controllers achieve ZBO. Under an unmodelled 30\% heat‑flux spike, however, the SAC agent obtains a cumulative reward 22\% higher than MPC because it learns to pre‑cool the tank prior to the spike—exactly the kind of anticipatory behaviour that MPC, constrained by a finite prediction horizon and linearised dynamics, cannot replicate. This gap quantitatively justifies the added complexity of the RL approach.

