Chapter 8

Validation Strategy Without Laboratory Experiments

This dissertation is theoretical and architectural; no physical calorimeter, injection test, or sub‑scale tank has been constructed. All validation is therefore performed against existing and future public flight datasets and through established numerical verification protocols.

1. ZBOT‑1 pressure‑rise data (2017–2018). The digital twin has been shown to match the measured pure‑fluid pressure‑rise curves to within \pm 7\% across multiple fill levels. This validates the nucleate boiling and interfacial transport physics in the absence of helium.
2. ZBOT‑NC noncondensable gas data (post‑2025). The Dalton–Henry sub‑model and the condensation blanketing correction factor will be refined and validated as soon as the data from the NG‑23 Cygnus mission are released. The model architecture has been deliberately constructed to accommodate these data without structural alteration.
3. McIntosh LBL verification. The MLI model is verified against published cryostat heat‑leak measurements from McIntosh’s original 1996 study, covering temperatures down to 77 K. The agreement is within the stated \pm 5\% band for all cases.
4. RL controller closed‑loop testing. The trained SAC policy is evaluated on the full‑order CFD twin (Star‑CCM+ coupled with MATLAB), which acts as a surrogate for real hardware. The safety shield is shown to prevent over‑pressure in every randomised scenario, including extreme heat‑flux spikes and sensor failures.

This strategy demonstrates mission readiness at the architectural level; a follow‑on programme of sub‑scale testing is recommended for flight qualification, but the mathematical framework is complete.

