Chapter 9

Conclusion and Mission Impact

The dissertation establishes a complete theoretical and technical architecture for zero‑boil‑off cryogenic propellant storage on Mars‑class missions. Five formal contributions are made:

1. A lower bound on passive MLI heat leak and a VD‑MLI design, optimised via the McIntosh LBL equations and simulated‑annealing with a POD‑GRNN surrogate, that achieves an 18 % performance gain over a uniform blanket of equal mass—consistent with the 21–25 % improvements reported for liquid‑hydrogen VD‑MLI in the recent literature.
2. A Dalton–Henry coupled thermodynamic model that correctly predicts the effect of helium pressurant on condensation and pressure rise, pre‑structured for validation against the ZBOT‑NC flight data.
3. An Integrated Tube‑on‑Shield structural‑thermal architecture that incorporates 3D‑printed active cooling channels and a Teflon‑based CTE‑slip layer, reducing conductive heat leak tenfold while maintaining structural integrity throughout all thermal cycles.
4. A dual‑redundant cryocooler scheme with a cross‑strapped helium loop and an FMECA‑verified reliability exceeding 0.95, together with a complete mass budget that demonstrates the active system is mass‑saving for any mission exceeding 22\,\text{t} of initial propellant.
5. A provably safe reinforcement‑learning controller, protected by a Lyapunov‑based safety shield, that quantitatively outperforms model‑predictive control under realistic disturbances. The controller can be embedded in any future cryogenic depot or transfer vehicle.

The capstone is the autonomous RL agent, underpinned by the rigorous thermal‑fluid theory and the detailed design specifications of the preceding chapters. The technology is shown to be mission‑enabling for the 111 t Mars‑return stage, preserving over 100 t of propellant that would otherwise be lost to boil‑off, and thereby rendering a crewed Mars return architecture both physically and programmatically viable.

