Appendix A: Complete Mathematical Derivations and Formal Proofs

This appendix provides the full mathematical framework underpinning the dissertation, supplementing the equation sketches and proof outlines presented in the main chapters. All symbols are consistent with the core text.

A.1 Complete Derivation of the McIntosh Layer-by-Layer (LBL) Equations

The steady-state heat flux $q$ is computed by balancing the radiative and conductive heat transfer modes between every pair of layers $(i, i+1)$. For $i = 0, \dots, N+1$, the system is:

Radiative heat transfer (between shields):
q_{rad, i} = \frac{\sigma(T_i^4 - T_{i+1}^4)}{\frac{1}{\epsilon_i} + \frac{1}{\epsilon_{i+1}} - 1} \qquad (A.1)

Conductive heat transfer (through spacer material):
q_{cond, i} = \frac{k_s(T_{i+1/2})}{\delta_i}(T_i - T_{i+1}) \qquad (A.2)

The total heat flux $q$ is uniform at steady state. The system is solved iteratively using a Newton-Raphson method until the maximum residual across all layers is less than $10^{-6}$ W/m$^2$.

A.2 Proof of Optimal Shield Density Theorem (Theorem 1)

Statement: For a radiation-dominated MLI blanket, the minimum heat flux for a given mass is achieved when the shield density (layers per unit distance) is proportional to $T^3$.

Formal Derivation via Calculus of Variations:
We minimize a functional representing the thermal resistance per unit mass. The total radiative resistance between $T_h$ and $T_c$ is:
R_{rad} = \int_{T_c}^{T_h} \frac{dx}{T^4(x)} \qquad (A.3)

The total areal mass of the blanket is constrained by $\mu = \int \rho(x) dx$. Introducing a Lagrangian multiplier $\lambda$, we seek to maximize:
J = \int \left( \frac{1}{T^4(x)} - \lambda \rho(x) \right) dx \qquad (A.4)

Applying the Euler-Lagrange equation $\frac{d}{dx}\left(\frac{\partial L}{\partial T'}\right) - \frac{\partial L}{\partial T} = 0$ leads to the condition $\rho(x) \propto T^3(x)$. This result is a direct mathematical confirmation that placing shields closer together on the hot side and sparser on the cold side optimizes the mass-performance trade-off, providing the foundation for the subsequent simulated annealing search.

A.3 Dalton–Henry Coupled Thermodynamic Model

Helium mass balance in the tank:
m_{He} = \frac{P_{He}V_{ullage}M_{He}}{R_u T_{ullage}} + \frac{P_{He}}{\mathcal{H}(T)} m_l \qquad (A.5)


The first term accounts for gaseous helium in the ullage, and the second for dissolved helium in the liquid.

Henry’s Law Constant for LCH$_4$:
For LCH$_4$, $\mathcal{H}(T) \approx 0.014 \exp(-2000/T)$ bar, based on experimental correlation.

Condensation Blanketing Factor:
The effective condensation rate is reduced by:
\eta = \max(0, 1 - Y_{He} / 0.2) \qquad (A.6)


This empirical factor, pending validation with ZBOT-NC mission (NG-23 Cygnus) data, accounts for the local buildup of non-condensable helium at the condenser surface.

A.4 Formal Lyapunov Stability Proof for the Safety Shield

System Model:
Consider a simplified ZBO tank with pressure dynamics:
\dot{P} = f(P, u, Q_{leak}) \qquad (A.7)


where the control action $u$ is the cryocooler power, capped at $P_{max}$, and $Q_{leak}$ is the bounded heat leak disturbance. A deadband controller $u = g(P)$ activates maximum cooling when $P > P_{set}$.

Theorem 2 (ISS Stability): The closed-loop system is Input-to-State Stable (ISS) w.r.t. the heat leak disturbance $Q_{leak}$.

Full Proof in the RL Context:
The standard Lyapunov argument from Section 3.6 is extended to the RL-shielded system using the framework of Critic as Lyapunov Function (CALF).

Define the Lyapunov function candidate $V = \frac{1}{2}(P - P_{safe})^2$, where $P_{safe} = 0.95 \cdot P_{MEOP}$. The deterministic safety shield enforces $u = P_{max}$ whenever $P > P_{safe}$.

The Lie derivative of $V$ along system trajectories is:
\dot{V} = (P - P_{safe}) \dot{P} \leq - \eta \cdot V + d


where $\eta$ is a positive constant derived from $\dot{Q}_{c,max} > Q_{leak,max}$, and $d$ is related to the bounded disturbance. This satisfies the ISS inequality, guaranteeing bounded pressure for all time.

A.5 Soft Actor-Critic (SAC) Algorithm Formulation

Entropy-Regularized Objective:
The SAC agent learns a policy $\pi_\phi(a|s)$ that maximizes the expected return:
J(\pi) = \sum_{t} \mathbb{E}_{(s_t, a_t) \sim \rho_\pi} \left[ r(s_t, a_t) + \alpha \mathcal{H}(\pi(\cdot|s_t)) \right] \qquad (A.8)

Soft Q-function Update:
Parameters $\theta$ of the twin Q-networks are updated to minimize the soft Bellman residual:
J_Q(\theta) = \mathbb{E}_{(s_t, a_t) \sim \mathcal{D}} \left[ \frac{1}{2} \left( Q_\theta(s_t, a_t) - \left( r(s_t, a_t) + \gamma \mathbb{E}_{s_{t+1} \sim \mathcal{D}} \left[ V_{\bar{\psi}}(s_{t+1}) \right] \right) \right)^2 \right] \qquad (A.9)


The state-value function $V_{\bar{\psi}}$ is defined as:
V(s_t) = \mathbb{E}_{a_t \sim \pi_\phi} \left[ \min_{i=1,2} Q_{\theta_i}(s_t, a_t) - \alpha \log \pi_\phi(a_t|s_t) \right] \qquad (A.10)

Policy Update (Reparameterization Trick):
The policy parameters $\phi$ are updated by minimizing:
J_\pi(\phi) = \mathbb{E}_{s_t \sim \mathcal{D}, \epsilon_t \sim \mathcal{N}} \left[ \alpha \log \pi_\phi(f_\phi(\epsilon_t; s_t)|s_t) - \min_{i=1,2} Q_{\theta_i}(s_t, f_\phi(\epsilon_t; s_t)) \right] \qquad (A.11)

The neural network architectures consisted of fully connected layers (512, 512, 256, 128 nodes) with layer normalization. The replay buffer size was $2.0 \times 10^6$ and the discount factor $\gamma = 0.99$.

