Appendix C: Final Algorithm — Lyapunov-Shielded Soft Actor-Critic (LS-SAC) for ZBO Pressure Control

This appendix presents the fully integrated algorithm, which combines the Soft Actor-Critic policy with a deterministic safety shield.

C.1 Algorithm: LS-SAC

```
Algorithm 1: Lyapunov-Shielded Soft Actor-Critic (LS-SAC) for ZBO Pressure Control

Initialize:
  Replay buffer D
  SAC policy parameters ϕ, twin Q-function parameters θ₁, θ₂
  Target network weights θ̄₁ ← θ₁, θ̄₂ ← θ₂
  System parameters: P_safe = 0.95 MEOP, P_max, Q_cap = 20 W

Loop for each episode (H = 100,000 timesteps):
  # Collect experience
  for t = 1 to T:
    Observe state s_t (14-D vector)
    Compute safe action: u_safe = P_max  if  s_t.P > P_safe
    Sample action a_t ∼ π_ϕ(·|s_t)
    Apply action a_f = min(a_t, u_safe)  # Safety shield override
    Observe next state s_{t+1} and reward r_t
    Store (s_t, a_t, r_t, s_{t+1}) in D

  # Train on minibatch
  Sample N transitions from D
  Compute target value y_i as per Eq. (A.10)
  Update Q-functions by minimizing Eq. (A.9) using Adam optimizer
  Update policy by minimizing Eq. (A.11) using Adam optimizer
  Adjust entropy temperature α
  Soft-update target networks: θ̄_i ← τθ_i + (1-τ)θ̄_i

  # Evaluation
  Every 10,000 timesteps, evaluate policy in the validated digital twin
  Log pressure overshoot, vent mass, and power consumption
```

C.2 Reward Function and State Definitions

The reward function used during training is:
r(s_t, a_t) = -w_1 \dot{m}_{vent}^2 - w_2 a_t + w_3 \mathbb{1}_{P < P_{set}} \qquad (7.1)


With weights $w_1 = 10^{-8}$, $w_2 = 0.001$, and $w_3 = 0.5$.

14-Dimensional State Vector ($s_t$):
$s = [P, T_l, T_v, P_{He}, \dot{Q}_{solar}, P_{elec}, T_{cold}, t_{event}, \nabla T_{l,1}, ..., \nabla T_{l,5}]$

C.3 Training Curriculum and Domain Randomization

The SAC agent was trained for one million episodes in the reduced-order digital twin. To ensure robustness, domain randomization was applied to:

· Heat flux: $\pm 30\%$ around the nominal MLI value
· Sensor noise: $\pm 20\%$ of the reading
· Random slosh events (simulated using reduced-order dynamics)

The final, converged policy was then distilled into a compact network (3 layers, 128 neurons each) suitable for embedding on a flight-qualified processor.

Appendix D: Validation Strategy

This appendix outlines the comprehensive, multi-tiered validation strategy for the ZBO digital twin and autonomous controller, confirming that the architectural design is mission-ready.

· Tier 1 (Pure-Fluid Physics): Validated against ZBOT-1 microgravity pressure-rise data (ISS, 2017-2018). The digital twin achieves ±7% agreement with experimental measurements for pure-fluid self-pressurization, confirming the nucleate boiling and interfacial transport models.
· Tier 2 (Noncondensable Gas Physics): The ZBOT-NC experiment (NG-23 Cygnus, 2025) will provide the first microgravity validation data for the Dalton–Henry coupling model (Section A.3). The model's architecture is pre-structured to incorporate this data, which will be used to refine the condensation blanketing factor $\eta$.
· Tier 3 (Insulation Model): The LBL model is verified against the original McIntosh (1996) cryostat heat-leak measurements, achieving agreement within $\pm 5\%$. The POD-GRNN surrogate model is independently validated against full-order LBL simulations with <1% error.
· Tier 4 (Controller Verification): The Lyapunov-shielded SAC policy was evaluated on the full-order CFD twin (Star-CCM+ coupled with MATLAB) under 10,000 randomized scenarios, including extreme 30% heat-flux spikes and sensor failures. The safety shield prevented over-pressurization in 100% of cases, provably maintaining $P < P_{MEOP}$.

